    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/li-jinzhong"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-jinzhong.jpg" alt="Li Jinzhong" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/li-jinzhong">Li Jinzhong</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khitan_Rebellion.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae arose from the wreckage of Goguryeo, the northern Korean kingdom destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> and Silla in 668. Tang resettled many Goguryeo families around Yingzhou in Liaoxi, west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a>, alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribesmen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a>. When the Khitan chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-jinzhong">Li Jinzhong</a> rebelled against Tang in 696, the Goguryeo leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-jung-sang">Dae Jung-sang</a> and the Mohe chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geolsa-biu">Geolsa Biu</a> led their followers east. Both died during the flight, and Dae Jung-sang's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a> took command of the combined Goguryeo and Mohe groups. In 698 he defeated a pursuing Tang army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kaigu">Li Kaigu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tianmenling">Tianmenling</a> and founded a state named Jin near Mount Dongmo, close to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhua">Dunhua</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin province</a>. Whether Dae Joyeong himself was of Goguryeo or Mohe origin is disputed, since the Chinese histories describe him in different ways. His state grew by absorbing Goguryeo remnants and Mohe tribes, and he sent envoys to the Eastern Turks to strengthen his position against Tang. In 713 the Tang court, seeking peace on its north-eastern frontier, recognised him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commandery">Commandery</a> Prince of Bohai, and the name Bohai, Balhae in Korean, replaced Jin. By his death in 719 the kingdom controlled much of eastern Manchuria.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tang Dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang Dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tang_outline_map,_661.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kanguole, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae society was led by an aristocracy in which families of Goguryeo descent held most high offices, while much of the population, especially in the north, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a>; the proportions are debated and bear on modern arguments about the kingdom's identity. The economy combined farming, livestock raising, hunting, fishing and crafts suited to a cold northern climate. Chinese records praise the horses of Solbin, the iron of Wiseong and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> of Noseong, and the kingdom produced cloth of hemp and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a> of sable, tiger and bear, together with honey and ginseng, were its most valued exports. Trade moved along official routes radiating from the capitals: a road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> and by sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, a route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from the east coast, a road to Silla and routes to the Khitan and the Mohe of the far north. Some thirty-four Balhae embassies sailed to Japan between 727 and 919, exchanging furs for silks and other goods, and their envoys joined Japanese courtiers in composing Chinese poetry. The elite wrote literary Chinese and practised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>, building temples, stone lanterns and pagodas, while underfloor heating and roof-tile designs found at Balhae sites show continuities with Goguryeo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae's early survival depended on its army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-joyeong">Dae Joyeong</a>'s victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tianmenling">Tianmenling</a> in 698 allowed the state to form, and under his son King Mu (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mu-of-balhae">Dae Muye</a>, r. 719–737) it expanded aggressively. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heishui-mohe">the Heishui Mohe</a> on the Amur placed themselves under Tang protection, Mu ordered an attack on them, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-munye">Dae Munye</a>, who opposed the war, fled to the Tang court. In 732 a Balhae fleet under the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jang-mun-hyu">Jang Mun-hyu</a> crossed the sea and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dengzhou">Dengzhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, killing the Tang prefect. Tang responded by asking Silla to strike from the south in 733, but the Silla army turned back in heavy snow, and Balhae's relations with Tang soon settled into a peaceful tributary relationship. The central army was organised into guards on the Tang model, and provincial forces garrisoned fortified towns and mountain strongholds in a landscape of forests and river valleys. Under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seon-of-balhae">Seon</a> (r. 818–830) campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes of the north extended Balhae rule towards the Amur and Ussuri rivers. After this, records of warfare become scarce for most of the ninth century. When the Khitan armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Yelü Abaoji</a> invaded in 925, Balhae's frontier fortresses and capital fell within weeks, suggesting that the kingdom's military strength had declined during the long peace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/departement"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/departement.jpg" alt="Département" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/departement">Département</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_d%C3%A9partementale.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nilstilar, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae adapted the Tang system of government to its own needs. At the centre stood three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, the Jeongdangseong (Department of Political Affairs), the Seonjoseong and the Jungdaeseong, with the Jeongdangseong supervising six ministries named after Confucian virtues: Chung, In, Ui, Ji, Ye and Sin. The territory was divided into five capitals, fifteen prefectures (<em>bu</em>) and sixty-two districts (<em>ju</em>), though in outlying areas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> chiefs governed their own people under Balhae overlordship. King Mun (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mun-of-balhae">Dae Heummu</a>, r. 737–793), the longest-reigning king, built much of this structure. He moved the court from the original capital to the Central Capital and, around 756, to the Upper Capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanggyeong">Sanggyeong</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudan-river">Mudan River</a> valley near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ning-an">Ning'an</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilongjiang">Heilongjiang</a>. Laid out in a grid on the plan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, with an outer wall about 16 kilometres long, it was among the largest cities of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Mun later moved to the Eastern Capital, and the court returned to Sanggyeong in 794. Balhae kings were invested by the Tang emperor, from 762 as kings of the Bohai state, but at home they used their own reign titles, and the epitaph of Mun's daughter Princess Jeonghyo refers to him with imperial-style terms. In letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, kings sometimes presented themselves as heirs of Goguryeo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty.jpg" alt="Liao dynasty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liao_circuits.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SY, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and the north of the Korean peninsula for more than two centuries, standing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a>, the Khitan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe peoples</a> and Silla. Its adoption of Tang institutions, urban planning and literary culture carried Chinese models deep into the north-east, and the Tang called it the 'flourishing land of the east' (<em>Haedong seongguk</em>), a name associated with the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seon-of-balhae">Seon</a>. Its embassies formed one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s main channels to the continent in the eighth and ninth centuries: Japanese records preserve the letters exchanged and the names of Balhae envoys, and the Tang <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanming-calendar">Xuanming calendar</a>, adopted in Japan in 862, arrived with a Balhae embassy. After its fall its people and traditions passed to several successors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Khitan Liao</a> state absorbed most of its territory and employed former Balhae officials, refugees strengthened the new Korean kingdom of Goryeo, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a>, who later founded the Jin dynasty, descended from Mohe peoples of the region. Balhae's place in history is contested today. Korean historians, following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-deuk-gong">Yu Deuk-gong</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balhaego">Balhaego</a></em> of 1784, treat it as a Korean state that formed, with Unified Silla, a period of Northern and Southern States, whereas Chinese historians generally describe it as a regional regime within the Tang order.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liaoyang.jpg" alt="Liaoyang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E8%BE%BD%E9%98%B3%E7%99%BD%E5%A1%94%E5%85%AC%E5%9B%AD09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">红烧腰果, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Balhae's last century is poorly documented, but the sources point to growing internal strain. Succession disputes after King Mun's death in 793 brought several short reigns, and in the later ninth century the kingdom's hold over outlying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> tribes appears to have loosened. The collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> in 907 removed the regional order within which Balhae had flourished and coincided with the rise of the Khitan under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-liao">Yelü Abaoji</a>, who united the tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-river">Liao River</a> basin and proclaimed himself emperor in 916. Balhae and the Khitan clashed over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> region, and late in 925 Abaoji launched a full-scale invasion. His army took the fortress of Buyeo on the western frontier and besieged the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanggyeong">Holhan Fortress</a> at Sanggyeong, where the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-inseon">Dae Inseon</a>, surrendered early in 926. Abaoji turned the kingdom into the client state of Dongdan under his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-bei">Yelü Bei</a>, and much of its population was later moved west towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoyang">Liaoyang</a>. Resistance continued in local revolts and in short-lived restoration states such as Jeongan, while many nobles fled to Goryeo, which received the crown prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dae-gwang-hyeon">Dae Gwang-hyeon</a> in 934. A once popular theory that a great eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-paektu">Mount Paektu</a> destroyed Balhae has been ruled out, since the eruption has been dated to around 946.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Balhae, founded by Goguryeo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohe">Mohe</a> refugees in 698, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> for over two centuries, built Tang-style institutions and capitals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanggyeong">Sanggyeong</a>, linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> with the continent through its embassies, and remains central to Korean and Chinese debates about the history of the north-east.</em></p>

